# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# openclaw-refresh-keys.service — re-bridge the Windows credentials into the
# systemd user manager, once per boot.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (2026-09-23)
#
# The bridged credentials live in /dev/shm (tmpfs), so a reboot wipes them, and the
# user-manager environment is then rebuilt at boot from the STATIC
# ~/.config/environment.d/90-openclaw.conf alone. Until a human runs
# `oc refresh-keys`, every bridged name that drop-in does not carry (or carries at
# a stale value) is absent or wrong for anything the manager starts — including the
# gateway, which resolves env-backed SecretRefs at activation. That is the gap this
# unit closes: the refresh now happens by itself, once per boot.
#
# WITHOUT IT: a gateway-hosted run that starts before any bridged shell sees the
# stale set, and the only cue is a `key_cache MISSING` row in `oc health` — which
# nobody reads at boot.
#
# CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT: the refresh pushes the manager env and, when that CHANGED
# the gateway's environment (it does after a reboot, because /dev/shm's
# .hash/.resolved markers are gone), it restarts the gateway once. That restart is
# the command's own designed outcome, not a fault; if the unit happens to run before
# the gateway is up, no restart is needed and none is issued.
#
# NOT ENABLED BY install.sh — deliberately. install.sh links systemd/* into
# ~/.config/systemd/user/ and runs daemon-reload, and this repo has never enabled a
# unit from there (nothing in it calls `systemctl --user enable`), so enabling stays
# an explicit act:
#
#     systemctl --user enable openclaw-refresh-keys.service
#
# Verify without starting it:
#   systemd-analyze --user verify %h/.config/systemd/user/openclaw-refresh-keys.service
# What it did last boot:
#   journalctl --user -u openclaw-refresh-keys.service -b
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[Unit]
Description=Re-bridge the Windows credentials into the systemd user manager (once per boot)
Documentation=file:%h/github-wsl/ubuntu-console/README.md
# ORDERING (2026-10-17): Before=openclaw-gateway.service.  This file used to argue the
# opposite - "OpenClaw owns that unit, not this repo, so naming it here would be an
# ordering against something this tree cannot keep valid" - and that argument cost a real
# outage.  Measured after the 10:48:51 WSL restart: systemd started the gateway at
# 10:48:56, and THIS unit issued a gateway restart at 10:49:01, five seconds in.  The
# restart stacked because the guard in scripts/09d-oc-agents.sh reads `is-active`, which
# reports `active` the moment a Type=simple process spawns - so a gateway still minutes
# from serving looked settled.  The stacked stop left two MainThread processes behind,
# they held /home/wayne/.openclaw/state/openclaw.sqlite in "offline maintenance", and the
# next two starts were REFUSED: three attempts and ~13 minutes to serve.
#
# Bridging the env BEFORE the gateway starts removes the restart entirely - the manager
# env the gateway inherits is already the refreshed one, so nothing needs re-reading.  The
# old objection still holds in one direction: if OpenClaw renames its unit, this ordering
# silently goes inert.  That is why the script-side guard still has to be hardened; an
# inert ordering is the PREVIOUS behaviour, not a new failure mode.
After=network.target
Before=openclaw-gateway.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The same command the console runs. `oc refresh-keys` is a shell function, so
# tac-exec is what a unit calls (the documented way to reach the console library
# non-interactively) — which also means this unit never carries a second copy of
# the refresh logic.
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/tac-exec oc refresh-keys
# The refresh bridges from Windows over WSL interop (20s pwsh timeout), syncs
# SecretRefs, pushes the manager env and may restart the gateway. Give it room.
TimeoutStartSec=180
# A STOP IS NOT A FAULT, AND IT MUST NOT BE REPORTED AS ONE (2026-11-09).
#
# Measured: of the 68 recorded starts of this unit, exactly ONE ended in a signal —
# 2026-11-01T17:22:17Z, `Main process exited, code=killed, status=15/TERM` then
# `Failed with result 'signal'`. Its last log line was "Gateway [restart issued —
# waiting up to 120s for it to serve]": an operator stop landed inside this unit's own
# DESIGNED wait, and systemd filed the result next to units that had genuinely broken.
# A shutdown does the same thing, because Type=oneshot has no chance to finish.
#
# A TIMEOUT IS STILL A FAILURE, and this directive does not hide it. Measured in a
# throwaway oneshot (TimeoutStartSec=3, ExecStart=/bin/sleep 30, this directive):
# `Result=timeout`, `ExecMainCode=2`, `ExecMainStatus=15`, and the unit WAS still in
# `systemctl --user --failed` — systemd kills with TERM but classifies the expiry as
# its own result, so swallowed-by-SIGTERM does not apply. Measured again for the stop
# itself: `inactive/dead`, `Result=success`, `ExecMainStatus=0`, absent from --failed.
#
# THE OBJECTION THAT HELD THIS BACK WAS WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN PREMISE. It read: "TERM
# cannot distinguish an operator stop from a mid-write kill, and this is the only
# re-derive path for gateway.systemd.env." This unit does not write that file at all
# — `__oc_sync_gateway_env_file` step 2 refuses to, precisely because OpenClaw
# fingerprints its own unit; the file's mtime is 2026-11-07 16:36:14, days before
# this unit last ran. What it does write is `systemctl --user set-environment` (one
# atomic, idempotent call per name) plus the two hash/set markers, and those are
# written ONLY after every push succeeded. So an interruption cannot be recorded as
# a success: the markers stay unwritten and the next run retries. The design already
# tolerates being cut; the directive only stops it from being called broken.
#
# SIGSEGV, SIGKILL and any non-zero exit are untouched by this line, so a real crash
# still lands in --failed.
SuccessExitStatus=SIGTERM

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHICH SURFACE WINS (2026-11-09) — the precedence the 2026-11-01 HF drift exposed,
# written down because nothing recorded it and a day was spent on the consequence.
#
# Four surfaces can hold a credential. They do not have equal authority:
#
#   1. Windows User environment       the canonical SOURCE. The /dev/shm bridge cache
#                                     is a copy of it, wiped by a reboot and re-read
#                                     by this unit.
#   2. systemd user-manager env       DERIVED — this unit pushes into it. Read by
#                                     everything the manager starts EXCEPT the gateway.
#   3. ~/.openclaw/gateway.systemd.env  DERIVED, and the AUTHORITY for the gateway for
#                                     every name in the gateway unit's
#                                     OPENCLAW_SERVICE_MANAGED_ENV_KEYS (23 names,
#                                     2026-11-09). It is an EnvironmentFile= on that
#                                     unit, and an EnvironmentFile OVERRIDES an
#                                     inherited manager variable of the same name.
#   4. ~/.config/environment.d/       absent on this box (2026-11-09): contributes nothing.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE — for a MANAGED key, a disagreement between (2) and (3) is not two
# sources of truth. It is one source and one stale copy, and the gateway silently runs
# the stale one. Measured 2026-11-01: HUGGINGFACE_TOKEN was 37 chars `hf_…` in the
# manager and 825 chars `hf_oauth…` in the file, and the gateway held the 825-char
# value. Correcting the manager alone would NOT have fixed it; the FILE had to change.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-11-09 (lengths and sha prefixes only, never values): HUGGINGFACE_TOKEN
# and DEEPSEEK_API_KEY agree across (2) and (3) — 37/37 and 35/35, matching sha.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY is ABSENT from (2) and present in (3) at 73 chars: that is the
# DESIGNED arrangement for a managed key, not a fault, and must not be "repaired" by
# pushing it into the manager.
#
# The 24 names the run reports as ON NO SURFACE are a different population again:
# bridged in Windows, named by no config ref, present on none of (2), (3) or (4). They
# are waiting for a consumer, not broken.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
